 primary transport protocols used in the Internet. They differ fundamentally in how they handle data delivery, reliability, and speed.\n\nHere is a detailed comparison based on your requested criteria:\n\n### 1. Guarantees\n\n| Feature | TCP | UDP |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Reliability** | **Guaranteed.** TCP ensures that data is delivered correctly and in order. It uses acknowledgments (ACKs) and retransmissions. | **No guarantee.** UDP sends packets without checking if they arrive. It is \"fire and forget.\" |\n| **Ordering** | **Guaranteed.** Packets are delivered in the exact order they were sent. | **No guarantee.** Packets may arrive out of order or not at all. |\n| **Connection** | **Connection-oriented.** Requires a handshake (SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK) before data transfer. | **Connectionless.** No handshake; data is sent immediately. |\n| **Flow Control** | **Yes.** TCP manages congestion and ensures the receiver can handle the data rate. | **No.** UDP does not manage flow control. |\n\n###", "client": {"ttft_s": 0.6515252999961376, "decode_s": 4.749278200004483, "total_s": 5.401093599997694, "n_gen": 256, "n_chunks": 256, "tg": 53.692369505698636, "pp": 46.04579438461998}}
